      "dom_id": "question-149565",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Solve the inequality $-5z\\le -1.$",
      "worked_solution": "The variable $z$ is being *multiplied* by $-5.$ To isolate $z,$ we perform the *opposite* operation, which is *dividing* by $-5.$ Remember to perform the same operation on both sides.\n\nAlso, when multiplying or dividing an inequality by a negative number, we need to flip the inequality sign:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-5z\\,\\, & \\le \\,\\,-1 \\\\ \\frac{-5z}{-5}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{-1}{-5} \\\\ \\frac{-5z}{-5}\\,\\, & \\ge \\,\\,\\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can cancel a common factor of $-5$ from both the numerator and denominator.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{-5z}{-5} & \\ge \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ \\frac{\\cancel{-5}z}{\\cancel{-5}} & \\ge \\frac{1}{5} \\\\ z & \\ge \\frac{1}{5}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is $z\\ge \\frac{1}{5}.$",
